# Append to /etc/fstab: the writable footage partition (3rd partition on the
# card; OS partition goes read-only via `raspi-config` > Performance > Overlay FS).
# cli/dashberry-install appends this automatically (by PARTUUID) when it builds
# the card — this snippet stays as the manual-install reference.
# nodelalloc + data=journal + commit=1: an append allocates its blocks at
# write() time, which puts it in the journal transaction the 1 s commit is
# about to close — so a hard power cut (every ignition-off, and an impact that
# kills 12 V) loses ~1-2 s of the open segment. nodelalloc is LOAD-BEARING in
# that chain: with ext4's default delayed allocation, appended pages sit
# outside every transaction until writeback (30 s dirty_expire, 5 s flusher
# pass), commit=1 has nothing to order against, and a power cut leaves the open
# segment as it was at the last writeback — 0 BYTES on the card for a tail
# younger than ~30 s, for all that filesink wrote it unbuffered. Closed
# segments do not depend on this line: each recorder fsyncs a segment (and
# the directory entry) the moment splitmuxsink closes it. This line is about
# the segment being written when the 12 V dies, which is the one with the
# impact on it.
#
# data=journal, NOT data=ordered (changed 2026-08-09), because ordered mode
# left INTERIOR holes in every unclosed segment. Ordered mode's guarantee is
# "a block's data reaches the card before the metadata that makes it
# reachable" — and that covers a block's FIRST write only. Appending never
# lands on a 4 KB boundary, so every 1 s commit pushes out a partially-filled
# tail page with its remainder zeroed; completing that page is a SECOND write
# to an already-reachable block, which ordered mode does not order and the
# commit does not force. The result is a skew: the committed i_size says the
# data is there while the page on the card is still the zero-tailed version.
# Journaling the data removes the skew — page content and i_size land in the
# same transaction — so the file on the card is always a PREFIX of what was
# written: losses only ever at the tail, never in the middle.
#
# Measured before the change (cam/front_bad_last.ts, 2026-08-08): 7 holes in
# the last 17.0 s of a 48.9 s final segment, 14,721 zeroed bytes = 0.11% of
# the file, costing 210 frames — 7.0 s of video. Every hole ended exactly on
# a 4096-byte boundary and every one landed inside that second's IDR (see
# usr/local/bin/front-rec on why the two 1 Hz clocks phase-lock), and losing
# an IDR costs its whole GOP. rear_bad_final.ts: 3 holes, 3.0 s of a 3.9 s
# segment. The MPEG-TS container is what kept these files playable at all —
# the reader resyncs on the next 188-byte packet. The same holes in
# fragmented MP4 destroyed a whole GOP each AND, once one landed on the
# header, hid the entire file (crash tail, 2026-08-07).
#
# The costs, accepted: data=journal writes every byte twice (once to the
# journal, once to its final block), so the card sees ~2x the write volume —
# ~1 MB/s for both cameras against a class-10 card's tens of MB/s, and the
# extra wear is the price of the segment being readable. It also DEPENDS on
# the card honouring cache-flush/FUA, which cheap cards are known to lie
# about; that is not verified on this hardware, and it is why front-rec's
# periodic fsync (which narrows the same window from the other side) stays
# rather than being replaced by this line. NOT VERIFIED against a real power
# pull yet — the bench test is in README.md's table.
/dev/mmcblk0p3  /data  ext4  defaults,noatime,nodelalloc,data=journal,commit=1,nofail  0 2
